---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-a`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin A Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: { '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Vitamin A deficiency' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Vitamin A', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin A: Retinol, Beta-Carotene, Deficiency & Toxicity"
  description="Vitamin A reference — preformed retinol vs beta-carotene, vision and epithelial health, night blindness, teratogenicity, and safe dosing."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/vitamin-a", label: "Vitamin A" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin A</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitamin A is a group of fat-soluble compounds essential for vision (retinal in the
      rhodopsin cycle), epithelial cell differentiation, immune function, and reproduction.
      It exists as <em>preformed retinol</em> in animal foods and as <em>carotenoid
      precursors</em> in plants — chiefly beta-carotene, which the body converts to retinol
      as needed. It has the smallest safety margin of any vitamin: chronic excess is
      teratogenic and hepatotoxic.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements (retinol activity equivalents)</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adult men:</strong> 900 µg RAE/day (~3,000 IU).</li>
      <li><strong>Adult women:</strong> 700 µg RAE/day (~2,333 IU).</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 770 µg RAE/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 1,300 µg RAE/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Tolerable Upper Intake Level:</strong> 3,000 µg RAE/day (10,000 IU) — pertains to preformed retinol only; beta-carotene has no UL.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deficiency</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Rare in the developed world; still a leading cause of childhood blindness in low-income
      countries. Early sign is <strong>night blindness</strong> (nyctalopia) — impaired dark
      adaptation. Progressive damage: xerophthalmia, Bitot's spots, corneal ulceration,
      permanent blindness. Immunodeficiency and increased infection risk (particularly
      measles) accompany deficiency.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Toxicity — the safety margin is narrow</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Acute:</strong> single doses &gt; 200,000 IU cause nausea, vomiting, headache.</li>
      <li><strong>Chronic:</strong> intakes &gt; 25,000 IU/day for months cause liver damage, bone pain, alopecia, dry skin, and hypercalcaemia.</li>
      <li><strong>Teratogenic:</strong> &gt; 10,000 IU/day preformed retinol in pregnancy causes birth defects. Isotretinoin (a retinoid drug) is strictly contraindicated in pregnancy.</li>
      <li><strong>Bone fracture risk:</strong> observational data suggest chronic intakes &gt; 3,000 µg RAE/day are associated with reduced bone mineral density.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      Beta-carotene from food does not cause vitamin A toxicity because the conversion to
      retinol is regulated. However, <strong>high-dose beta-carotene supplements</strong>
      (&gt; 20 mg/day) increase lung cancer risk in smokers (CARET and ATBC trials) —
      avoid supplemental beta-carotene if you smoke.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Preformed retinol:</strong> beef liver (a single 3-oz serving contains ~9× the
      RDA — do not eat regularly), fish liver oils, egg yolk, dairy fat.
      <br /><strong>Beta-carotene:</strong> sweet potato, carrots, dark leafy greens (spinach,
      kale), butternut squash, cantaloupe. Cooking with a little fat improves absorption.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Supplementation</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Most multivitamins provide 100% of the RDA (750–900 µg RAE) — appropriate for most adults.</li>
      <li>Don't add a stand-alone vitamin A supplement unless prescribed for a documented deficiency.</li>
      <li>Do not take cod liver oil AND a vitamin A supplement — cod liver oil can contain 4,500 IU per teaspoon.</li>
      <li>Pregnancy: prenatal vitamins typically use beta-carotene rather than retinol for safety.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Vitamin A toxicity is a real risk with
      unmonitored high-dose supplementation. Consult a physician if you're considering
      any supplement above the RDA, particularly in pregnancy.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-a" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
